{"header":{"numFound":93,"maxScore":22.411955,"queryTime":654,"page":1,"pageSize":30},"results":[{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Kamiar Ahmadian","name":"Kamiar","surname":"Ahmadian","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Jalal Jalilian","name":"Jalal","surname":"Jalilian","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Alireza Pirzad","name":"Alireza","surname":"Pirzad","rank":3,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0106 biological sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"2. Zero hunger"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"15. Life on land"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"6. Clean water"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Nano-fertilizers improved drought tolerance in wheat under deficit irrigation","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["Abstract   Although, the deficit irrigation saves the water consumption, but it may cause the loss of yield. Recently, the nano-fertilizers have been used in the agriculture as an approach to minimize the adverse effects of drought stress. Thus, in this study, a two-year (2016/2017 and 2017/2018 winter wheat seasons) split-plot field experiment was conducted to investigate the effect of the nano-chelating fertilizers on the yield and water use efficiency of the wheat with three replications in Iran. The main plots consisted of full and deficit irrigation (50% of full irrigation requirement), and the subplots included foliar spraying with nano chelated -Silicon, -Boron, and -Zinc fertilizers and control. The total irrigation water was equal to 457 and 228 mm for full and deficit irrigation, respectively for the first year, and it was equal to 378 and 190 mm, respectively for the second year. Deficit irrigation significantly decreased the yield components, relative water content (RWC), and cell membrane stability index (CMSI) leading to reduction of the grain yield by 43%, but it increased the grain protein, WUE, and superoxide dismutase (SOD) and catalase (CAT) activities. Nano-zinc increased the protein percentage of wheat grain in both years and irrigation regimes. The activity of SOD and CAT was improved especially in the deficit -irrigated plants and also the yield, yield components, RWC, and CMSI of wheat by applying the nano-fertilizers under both irrigation conditions. Nano-silica application under deficit irrigation in both years resulted in 28% and 32% increase in the grain yield, respectively, compared to the control. The highest WUE was obtained using the nano-silica under deficit irrigation condition in both experiments. Generally, the results indicated the considerable importance of the nano-silica fertilizer in reducing the damage caused by the deficit irrigation and improving the growth characteristics of the wheat."],"publicationDate":"2021-02-01","publisher":"Elsevier BV","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_14cb","label":"CLOSED","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Agricultural Water Management","issnPrinted":"0378-3774","issnOnline":null,"issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":"106544","vol":"244","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_dedup___::43d20afd11cc1327a085f9973e3168c5","originalIds":["S0378377420320916","10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544","50|doiboost____|43d20afd11cc1327a085f9973e3168c5","3092060083"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":147.0,"influence":4.8644724E-9,"popularity":9.883107E-8,"impulse":87.0,"citationClass":"C3","influenceClass":"C4","impulseClass":"C3","popularityClass":"C3"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Urmia University","acronym":"Urmia University","id":"openorgs____::4ac0f8f5eb4b1bdc9e76acfa96130502","pids":[{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q3874128"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000404428645"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"5888970"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100007298"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"38476204"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"3529631"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.412763.5"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q3874128"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100007298"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/032fk0x53"}],"countries":[{"code":"IR","label":"Iran (Islamic Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.urmia.ac.ir/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"},{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"}],"license":"Elsevier TDM","accessRight":{"code":"c_14cb","label":"CLOSED","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":null},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"],"publicationDate":"2021-02-01","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn___print::258a378f6795ec3fbe5806e6b0fa6835","value":"Agricultural Water Management"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3092060083"},{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3092060083"},{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2020.106544"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Adrian Chummac","name":"Adrian","surname":"Chummac","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::3ad4e0995175c29f91ada390e732752c","fullName":"Brunella Bonaccorso","name":"Brunella","surname":"Bonaccorso","rank":2,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-6973-6425"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Mitigating Drought-Induced Yield Loss Risk for Durum Wheat through Optimized Planting Schedule and Irrigation in Southern Italy","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>The Mediterranean cropping systems continue to face drought risk, with the changing climate intensifying its effects. Adaptation strategies are essential and must be assessed for their efficacy to guarantee their sustainability and relevance in specific regions, thereby enhancing adoption and optimizing resource utilization. This study investigated two climate adaptation strategies, namely shifting planting date (mid-November, late-November, or early-December) and adjusted irrigation (full, 75%, 50%, 25% deficit irrigation (DI), and rainfed), in reducing drought-induced yield loss risk (i.e., the magnitude of the yield loss and the likelihood of its occurrence) for durum wheat in the two provinces of Basilicata (Matera and Potenza) in southern Italy.The study employed crop and probabilistic modeling to simultaneously assess the effectiveness of shifting planting dates and irrigation adjustments while quantifying the yield loss risk caused by drought. The crop model, CSM-CERES Wheat, was utilized in simulating the yield at different management combinations initialized using the weather (1991–2023), soil, and management data. Copula functions were used to model the dependency structure between drought intensity and yield anomaly calculated from the simulated yield, allowing the assessment of drought-induced yield loss risk.Drought-induced yield loss risk in Matera was characterized by a relatively high likelihood of experiencing yield reductions with probabilities ranging from 36.6% to 78.4%, whereas Potenza exhibited negligible to low probabilities with the exception for early-December planting under rainfed condition (i.e., 55.9%). Shifting the planting date to mid-November and increasing irrigation levels reduced the likelihood of drought-induced yield loss, with significant results observed in Potenza. In Potenza, planting durum wheat in mid- to late-November under rainfed conditions showed a low risk of yield loss due to drought. This suggests that durum wheat can be grown with little or no irrigation. However, in Matera, even with full irrigation, yield loss risk was marginally high during intense dry conditions.In Matera under rainfed conditions, substantial yield loss estimates (i.e., 70% likelihood) for late-November and early-December planting were 0.1 and 0.7 t ha-1, respectively, for moderate drought severity (-0.84 &gt; spei ≥ -1.28), while for exceptional droughts (spei &lt; -2.33), yield loss estimates were 4.0 and 1.2 t ha⁻¹, respectively. In Potenza, substantial yield loss was only experienced for early-December planting under rainfed conditions during extreme  (-1.65 &gt; spei ≥ -2.33) to exceptional drought, with estimated yield losses of 0.1 and 0.5 t ha⁻¹, respectively.The results demonstrated that shifting the planting date to mid-November and implementing deficit and full irrigation largely mitigated drought‑induced yield loss risk in Basilicata, most notably in Potenza. Earlier planting and minimal irrigation in Potenza can help reduce yield loss risk due to drought. In Matera, on the other hand, early planting combined with full and deficit irrigation also mitigated the impact of drought except during extremely dry conditions, indicating additional or alternative adaptation strategies to cope with its impact.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2026-03-14","publisher":"Copernicus GmbH","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":null,"container":null,"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::9825e34cfdd943edbc8fa3c7ebb523c4","originalIds":["10.5194/egusphere-egu26-12892","50|doiboost____|9825e34cfdd943edbc8fa3c7ebb523c4"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.5194/egusphere-egu26-12892"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":2.6085654E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"University of Messina","acronym":"UNIME","id":"openorgs____::57316020e03f24759dce0fedeab4caa6","pids":[{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q1245318"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"112862951"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100008908"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100008908"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.10438.3e"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"2116104"},{"scheme":"OrgReg","value":"IT0033"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/05ctdxz19"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"999662601"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000121788421"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q1245318"}],"countries":[{"code":"IT","label":"Italy"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.unime.it/"},{"legalName":"Istituto Universitario Di Studi Superiori Di Pavia","acronym":"IUSS","id":"openorgs____::0b49cbc6da9cbd87aca7c21df0e6ee31","pids":[{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q3803694"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"997963355"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"2800920820"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q3803694"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/0290wsh42"},{"scheme":"OrgReg","value":"IT0053"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.30420.35"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"000000010724054X"}],"countries":[{"code":"IT","label":"Italy"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.iusspavia.it/"},{"legalName":"Central Luzon State University","acronym":"CLSU","id":"openorgs____::25be8146c72fd8d12d2f90f7a366af27","pids":[{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/02qf7df19"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"203854029"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q1053895"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"7978162"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000106643873"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.443260.7"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"957570421"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q1053895"}],"countries":[{"code":"PH","label":"Philippines"}],"websiteurl":"https://clsu.edu.ph/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.5194/egusphere-egu26-12892"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu26-12892"],"publicationDate":"2026-03-14","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Priyanka Shrivastava","name":"Priyanka","surname":"Shrivastava","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Hoshiyar Singh","name":"Hoshiyar","surname":"Singh","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"null Neetu","name":null,"surname":"Neetu","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Shubham C. Salve","name":"Shubham C.","surname":"Salve","rank":4,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Genotypic Performance of Wheat  (Triticum aestivum L.) under Contrasting Irrigation Regimes: Implications for  Water Use Efficiency and Productivity","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>This review synthesizes recent research on the effects of contrasting irrigation regimes on the genotypic performance of wheat, with particular emphasis on productivity and water use efficiency (WUE). Adequate irrigation during critical growth stages such as crown root initiation, flowering, and grain filling enhances plant growth, photosynthetic activity, biomass accumulation, and yield components. In contrast, water deficit conditions reduce stomatal conductance, canopy development, and grain formation, ultimately limiting yield potential. However, optimized irrigation strategies, including deficit irrigation and improved irrigation scheduling, can enhance WUE while maintaining acceptable yield levels. The review also highlights the significant role of genotypic variability in determining wheat performance under varying irrigation conditions. Wheat genotypes exhibit considerable variation in morphological and physiological traits such as root architecture, relative water content, chlorophyll stability, and stay-green characteristics, which contribute to improved drought tolerance and water productivity. Furthermore, genotype × environment interactions play a critical role in determining genotype adaptability across different irrigation regimes. Stability analysis methods such as regression models, AMMI analysis, and GGE biplot techniques are widely used to identify stable and high-performing genotypes under diverse environmental conditions. In addition, agronomic strategies including integrated irrigation and nutrient management, conservation agriculture practices, soil moisture conservation techniques, and crop residue management can significantly enhance wheat productivity under limited irrigation conditions. Integrating improved irrigation practices with the development of drought-tolerant wheat varieties will be essential for sustaining crop productivity under water-limited environments. Overall, adopting efficient irrigation management and selecting genotypes with higher water use efficiency are key strategies for achieving sustainable wheat production in the face of increasing water scarcity and climate change.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2026-03-30","publisher":"Sciencedomain International","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":null,"container":{"name":"Journal of Experimental Agriculture International","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"2457-0591","issnLinking":null,"ep":"329","iss":null,"sp":"310","vol":"48","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::9b728f8229d86da0e7c9994a39418caa","originalIds":["10.9734/jeai/2026/v48i44162","50|doiboost____|9b728f8229d86da0e7c9994a39418caa"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/jeai/2026/v48i44162"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":2.6085654E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":null,"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/jeai/2026/v48i44162"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.9734/jeai/2026/v48i44162"],"publicationDate":"2026-03-30","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn__online::ce461d1480bd829707743ce7659a92a1","value":"Journal of Experimental Agriculture International"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":null,"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Wang, Ziqian","name":"Ziqian","surname":"Wang","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Zhang, Bo","name":"Bo","surname":"Zhang","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Li, Jiahao","name":"Jiahao","surname":"Li","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Lian, Shihao","name":"Shihao","surname":"Lian","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Zhang, Jinshan","name":"Jinshan","surname":"Zhang","rank":5,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Shi, Shubing","name":"Shubing","surname":"Shi","rank":6,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"grain yield"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"deficit-regulated irrigation"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"soil moisture content"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"water-use efficiency"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"root-growth dynamics"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"winter wheat"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Effects of Deficit-Regulated Irrigation on Root-Growth Dynamics and Water-Use Efficiency of Winter Wheat in a Semi-Arid Area","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Water management is critical for wheat production under extreme drought conditions, and the mechanisms by which root dynamics and soil water utilization affect wheat yield are uncertain. This study was conducted in 2023–2024 under a mesophilic semi-arid climate with a two-factor partitioned experimental design, aiming to assess the response of different irrigation amounts in winter wheat crops on root growth and development, soil water utilization, and yields in different soil horizons. The results showed that variety and irrigation volume had significant effects on the spatial and temporal distribution of root and yield components, with irrigation volume having the greatest effect on yield. Compared with CK, deficit-regulated irrigation significantly promoted root penetration to deeper layers and delayed root senescence. DRWD, RLD, RSA, and RV decreased gradually with increasing soil depth, and the peaks of RLD, RSA, and RV appeared at the tassel to flowering stage, respectively; and under deficit-regulated irrigation, the contribution of the A2W4 treatment to stable yield was greater. Therefore, A2W4 is an effective water-saving irrigation method to improve grain yield and water-use efficiency under deficit-regulated irrigation.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2024-09-20","publisher":"MDPI AG","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref","Water","Volume 16","Issue 18","Pages: 2678"],"formats":["application/pdf"],"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Water","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"2073-4441","issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":"2678","vol":"16","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_dedup___::4625c48a34ec63967d25d27254c9f883","originalIds":["w16182678","10.3390/w16182678","50|doiboost____|4625c48a34ec63967d25d27254c9f883","oai:mdpi.com:/2073-4441/16/18/2678/","50|multidiscipl::c21436ba1a58e89fe37e402914365ce5"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/w16182678"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":18.0,"influence":2.6703277E-9,"popularity":1.3574579E-8,"impulse":18.0,"citationClass":"C4","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C4","popularityClass":"C4"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Xinjiang Agricultural University","acronym":"Xinjiang Agricultural University","id":"openorgs____::0521b511c5aff195c84b282b007b7a60","pids":[{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100012142"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q8044607"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"17941402"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04qjh2h11"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000093549799"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q8044607"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.413251.0"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"166427055"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100012142"}],"countries":[{"code":"CN","label":"China (People's Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.xjau.edu.cn/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::c2cdfa5866e03cdd07d313cbc8fb8311","value":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute"},{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/w16182678"}],"license":"CC BY","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.3390/w16182678"],"publicationDate":"2024-09-20","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::fdec620f68b36eb9d40dcd1651762540","value":"Water"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/w16182678"}],"license":"CC BY","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.3390/w16182678"],"publicationDate":"2024-09-20","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::fdec620f68b36eb9d40dcd1651762540","value":"Water"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::c2cdfa5866e03cdd07d313cbc8fb8311","value":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Mahdi Akbari","name":null,"surname":null,"rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Hamed Najafi Alamdarlo","name":null,"surname":null,"rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Seyed Habibollah Mosavi","name":null,"surname":null,"rank":3,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"fas/per","label":"Persian"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"Environmental sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"deficit irrigation"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"qazvin plain"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"water protection"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"TD169-171.8"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"GE1-350"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"drought"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"Environmental protection"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"risk"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Analysis of the effects of drought risk management using water productivity indicators","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["In recent years, with droughts and declining irrigation water, efficient use of water and water management has become inevitable. For this purpose, in this study, the risk effects of irrigation strategy in Qazvin plain in the 2015-2016 were analyzed. Initially, the percentage change in water quantity was calculated during the drought period. Then, using water-yield functions, deficit irrigation was applied to plants in four scenarios of 19, 32, 44 and 51% corresponding to the periods of weak, medium, severe and very severe droughts. Then, using the TM-PMP model, the risk effects of drought were investigated on the cropping pattern and water consumption productivity. The results showed that deficit irrigation increased the area under crops of wheat, barley and beans. This management strategy drastically reduces gross income by 24 percent in very severe drought conditions. Income risk is reduced by applying a deficit irrigation scenario during drought periods. Gross margin also decreases non-linearly as income risk. Water use efficiency decreased for wheat, barley, corn, tomato, maize and bean crops, and increased for beet and alfalfa crops. Therefore, if the irrigation strategy is implemented, the rate of income reduction in drought conditions will be 24%, but otherwise the income reduction will reach 58 percentages."],"publicationDate":"2021-05-01","publisher":"University of Sistan and Baluchestan","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["مخاطرات محیط طبیعی, Vol 10, Iss 27, Pp 197-209 (2021)"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"مخاطرات محیط طبیعی","issnPrinted":"2676-4377","issnOnline":null,"issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":null,"vol":null,"edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"dedup_wf_002::629861cfc62baffa66f10438685ea6dd","originalIds":["oai:doaj.org/article:25202b4dad9d4f42aff8c6677a1805f3","50|doajarticles::3d62361cee2090b9ba8ce1a0572557a2"],"pids":null,"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":1.272318E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":null,"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"driver______::bee53aa31dc2cbb538c10c2b65fa5824","value":"DOAJ"}],"instances":[{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.22111/jneh.2020.33550.1634"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doaj.org/article/25202b4dad9d4f42aff8c6677a1805f3"],"publicationDate":"2021-05-01","refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::d6c881eef6cd92494fbe43eb3efcc606","value":"مخاطرات محیط طبیعی"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"driver______::bee53aa31dc2cbb538c10c2b65fa5824","value":"DOAJ"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Rajaie Majid","name":"Rajaie","surname":"Majid","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Reza Soleimani","name":"Reza","surname":"Soleimani","rank":2,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid_pending","value":"0000-0003-4726-2107"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":null,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":null,"mainTitle":"Growth stimulants improve physiological performance, grain yield, and nutrient efficiency in wheat under optimal and deficit irrigation","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Growth stimulants (Gs) have recently gained attention to reduce chemical fertilizer overuse and enhance plant resilience to abiotic stresses. This study compared the impact of various Gs on wheat physiological traits, yield, and nutrient uptake under optimal and water-deficit irrigation. A two-year experiment (2020–2022) used a split-plot arrangement in a randomized complete block design with three replications. Two irrigation intervals (after 70 and 140 mm cumulative evaporation) were applied with seven Gs treatments: amino acids, humic acid, fulvic acid, seaweed extract, Azotobacter inoculation, their combination, and a control. Drought significantly reduced chlorophyll a (10.2%), chlorophyll b (18.6%), relative water content (4.7%), and membrane stability index (10.3%), but increased proline accumulation (85%). Drought reduced yield and its components, whereas Gs enhanced productivity under both irrigation regimes. Combined treatment raised chlorophyll a (24.3%), chlorophyll b (38.3%), RWC (21.1%), and MSI (27.2%), but lowered proline by 31.6% under drought, confirming effective stress alleviation. Under optimal irrigation, only the combined treatment improved grain yield (13.4%), whereas under drought, amino acids, fulvic acid, seaweed extract, and the combined treatment raised yield by 13.0%, 13.2%, 13.2%, and 15.5%, respectively. Drought significantly reduced nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, zinc, and copper in straw and grain; however, Gs enhanced macro- and micronutrient accumulation. Year effects were attributed to 33% higher rainfall in the first season, which affected physiological traits and nutrient uptake. These results underscore Gs application potential as a sustainable strategy to enhance physiological performance, nutrient efficiency, and grain yield in wheat, especially under water-limited conditions.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2026-01-01","publisher":"Elsevier BV","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":null,"container":null,"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::254bec35d5b6e17563d5c34791e240b2","originalIds":["10.2139/ssrn.7070745","50|doiboost____|254bec35d5b6e17563d5c34791e240b2"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.2139/ssrn.7070745"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":2.6085654E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":null,"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.2139/ssrn.7070745"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7070745"],"publicationDate":"2026-01-01","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":null,"otherTitles":null,"green":null,"inDiamondJournal":null,"isGreen":null,"isInDiamondJournal":null},{"authors":[{"id":"orcid_______::bd37170f0fbc9c53cdb2a9e2858b9e22","fullName":"Zeng, Ruiyun","name":"Ruiyun","surname":"Zeng","rank":1,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0001-7239-7308"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"Yao, Fengmei","name":"Fengmei","surname":"Yao","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Zhang, Sha","name":"Sha","surname":"Zhang","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Yang, Shanshan","name":"Shanshan","surname":"Yang","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Bai, Yun","name":"Yun","surname":"Bai","rank":5,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::76093d91e0e3b6319a63f182800461df","fullName":"Zhang, Jiahua","name":"Jiahua","surname":"Zhang","rank":6,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-2894-9627"},"provenance":null}},{"id":"orcid_______::25033040257a412567e8f00bdd1547de","fullName":"Wang, Jingwen","name":"Jingwen","surname":"Wang","rank":7,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0003-2556-7532"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"Wang, Xin","name":"Xin","surname":"Wang","rank":8,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":[{"code":"FR","label":"France","provenance":null}],"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0106 biological sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"[SDV.SA]Life Sciences [q-bio]/Agricultural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"2. Zero hunger"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"[SDV.SA] Life Sciences [q-bio]/Agricultural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"CERES-Wheat model"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"Water productivity"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"15. Life on land"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"630"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"6. Clean water"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"Winter wheat"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"North China Plain"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"13. Climate action"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"Deficit irrigation"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Assessing the effects of precipitation and irrigation on winter wheat yield and water productivity in North China Plain","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["Abstract   Achieving high crop yield with less irrigation is important to improve water productivity (WP) and irrigation water productivity (IWP) in water-limited regions in the North China Plain (NCP). Coupling the impacts of precipitation and irrigation on the crop yield is, therefore, an essential tool for understanding crop response to different water sources and forecasting irrigation water requirements. In this study, the CERES-Wheat model was used to simulate winter wheat yield, WP, and IWP under different irrigation schedules (no, deficit, full, and automatic irrigation) and three precipitation category years (wet, normal, and dry years). Results showed that the amount of precipitation fluctuated significantly over site-years, leading to considerably varied irrigation water requirements. For the wet year, deficit two irrigations at greening and jointing stages (treatment 11) alleviated water stress during key winter wheat growth periods, contributing to increasing biomass, yield, WP, and IWP. However, when reducing the irrigation amount by 50%, a significant increase in WP and a nonsignificant difference in yield were found at Fengqiu and Shangqiu. Two irrigation applications 11 improved pre-anthesis biomass and wheat yield while achieving high WP and IWP in normal and dry years (except for extreme drought conditions). The finding also indicated that the distribution of growing season precipitation exerted a significant impact on irrigation time. If the early season precipitation was low, shifting the irrigation to an earlier time to ensure pre-anthesis water requirements, which can synchronously achieve the goals of increasing WP and maintaining a higher yield. In conclusion, optimizing irrigation strategies to various precipitation conditions will be a promising and effective practice for wheat production and water conservation."],"publicationDate":"2021-10-01","publisher":"Elsevier BV","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":["Lucas, Nelly","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences Beijing (UCAS)","Chinese Academy of Sciences Beijing (CAS)","Qingdao University of Science and Technology","Environnement Méditerranéen et Modélisation des Agro-Hydrosystèmes (EMMAH)","Avignon Université (AU)-Institut National de Recherche pour l’Agriculture, l’Alimentation et l’Environnement (INRAE)","National Institute of Informatics (NII)","National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC) 42071425 41871253National Key Research and Development Program of China 2016YFD0300110\"Taishan Scholar\" Project of Shandong Province TSXZ201712"],"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_14cb","label":"CLOSED","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Agricultural Water Management","issnPrinted":"0378-3774","issnOnline":null,"issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":"107063","vol":"256","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_dedup___::7dc39c9adb359acc9973168ca6e01845","originalIds":["S0378377421003280","10.101