},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q985808"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000417604150"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.144022.1"}],"countries":[{"code":"CN","label":"China (People's Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"https://www.nwsuaf.edu.cn"},{"legalName":"China Agricultural University","acronym":"CAU","id":"openorgs____::5fb3dc7baf662c4bc6e2914e803e8143","pids":[{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q257655"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100003778"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100003778"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100002365"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100002365"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q257655"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000405308290"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04v3ywz14"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"52158045"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"919315"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"995210010"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.22935.3f"}],"countries":[{"code":"CN","label":"China (People's Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"http://english.cau.edu.cn/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"},{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1111/jac.12563"}],"license":"Wiley Online Library User Agreement","accessRight":{"code":"c_14cb","label":"CLOSED","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":null},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/jac.12563"],"publicationDate":"2021-10-19","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn___print::f544a27c94fb9fa4bce4a47a2c3923ae","value":"Journal of Agronomy and Crop Science"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1111/jac.12563"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3210880108"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.1111/jac.12563"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3210880108"},{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1111/jac.12563"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.1111/jac.12563"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Farahnaz Momtazi","name":"Farahnaz","surname":"Momtazi","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Hamidreza Miri","name":"Hamidreza","surname":"Miri","rank":2,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid_pending","value":"0000-0002-7792-1161"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"Barmak Jafari Haghighi","name":"Barmak","surname":"Jafari Haghighi","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Abdolreza Jafari","name":"Abdolreza","surname":"Jafari","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Hamidreza Ebrahimi","name":"Hamidreza","surname":"Ebrahimi","rank":5,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Agrophysiological responses of bread wheat to raised-bed planting and irrigation level","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:title>ABSTRACT</jats:title>                <jats:p>To investigate the response of wheat to drought stress under different plantation systems, two field experiments were carried out in 2021/22 and 2022/23 in Arsanjan, Iran. The main plot was three different plantation systems (flat planting, 60, and 120 cm raised beds) and the sub plots were irrigation levels at 100% (control), 80% (medium stress), and 60% (severe stress) of crop evapotranspiration. Raised bed planting increased grain yield by 24% compared to flat cultivation. This increase for biological yield was about 17%, the number of ears was 7%, and the number of seeds per ear was 21%. Drought stress caused a significant decrease in grain yield (46.4%), number of ears (20.5%), seed number (30.3%), biological yield (45.9%), chlorophyll a (31.8%), chlorophyll b (28.1%) and relative water content (5.3%) and increased, proline content (24.3%) and ion leakage (147.3%). Water use efficiency was increased in raised bed planting and deficit irrigation treatments. The decrease in yield due to drought stress was moderated to some extent in the raised bed system and therefore cultivation on raised beds is suggested as a suitable planting pattern to improve yield and WUE in wheat under conditions similar to the experimental area, especially under water shortage conditions.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2024-10-17","publisher":"IWA Publishing","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Water Supply","issnPrinted":"1606-9749","issnOnline":"1607-0798","issnLinking":null,"ep":"3707","iss":null,"sp":"3697","vol":"24","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::297b9ba8d1529fdf1506687dba050f30","originalIds":["10.2166/ws.2024.228","50|doiboost____|297b9ba8d1529fdf1506687dba050f30"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.2166/ws.2024.228"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":1.0,"influence":2.1954367E-9,"popularity":2.5828872E-9,"impulse":1.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Islamic Azad University of Arsanjan","acronym":"IAUA","id":"openorgs____::97a9c2195a5bb95cdeb3e3644f7225e9","pids":[{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q15228860"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/05fbv2v73"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"38027600"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.449216.c"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q15228860"}],"countries":[{"code":"IR","label":"Iran (Islamic Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.iaua.ac.ir/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.2166/ws.2024.228"}],"license":"CC BY","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.2166/ws.2024.228"],"publicationDate":"2024-10-17","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::d4fbbc314480ecdc0a17f78034c60606","value":"Water Supply"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Abdul Ghaffar","name":"Abdul","surname":"Ghaffar","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Niaz Hussain","name":"Niaz","surname":"Hussain","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Muhammad Nadeem","name":"Muhammad","surname":"Nadeem","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Zubeda Parveen","name":"Zubeda","surname":"Parveen","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Ali Ahmed","name":"Ali","surname":"Ahmed","rank":5,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Muhammad Tariq Javed","name":"Muhammad Tariq","surname":"Javed","rank":6,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Sikander Hayat","name":"Sikander","surname":"Hayat","rank":7,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0106 biological sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Impact of Foliar-Applied Osmolytes on Growth, Physiology and Yield of Wheat under Well-Watered and Drought Stressed Conditions","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Drought stress is one of the most critical abiotic constraint limiting wheat productivity in arid and semi-arid regions. A field experiment was conducted during the 2024-25 wheat season at the Arid Zone Research Institute (AZRI), Bhakkar, Punjab, Pakistan, to evaluate the role of selected osmolytes in enhancing wheat performance under well-watered and drought-stressed regimes. The treatments consisted of two soil moisture regimes: control (irrigation maintained at 65% available water) and moisture stress (irrigation maintained at 35% available water) and foliar application of water spray, sorbitol (15 mM), proline (20 mM), glycine betaine (25 mM), trehalose (10 mM), and γ-aminobutyric acid (GABA; 5 mM), along with a non-treated control. Drought stress markedly reduced plant height, tiller number, spike length, grains per spike, 1000-grain weight, and grain yield compared to well-watered conditions. However, osmolyte application significantly alleviated these reductions. Among the treatments, GABA (5 mM) exhibited the most pronounced effects by increasing plant height (75.33 cm), tiller density (300 m⁻²), spikelets per spike (17.00), and grains per spike (43.33) under drought conditions. GABA also improved 1000-grain weight (40.30 g), biological yield (10.33 t ha⁻¹), and grain yield (4.00 t ha⁻¹) relative to untreated drought-stressed plants. Moreover, GABA-treated plants maintained higher membrane stability index (75.67%), leaf water content (80.75%), and chlorophyll content (SPAD 34.50), demonstrating improved physiological resilience under stress. Other osmolytes, particularly sorbitol and trehalose, also provided moderate benefits. These results suggest that foliar-applied osmolytes, especially GABA, effectively mitigate the adverse effects of water deficit by sustaining growth, yield, and physiological integrity of wheat. Incorporation of such stress-alleviating agents in drought-prone regions of Pakistan could enhance wheat resilience and secure grain production under changing climate conditions.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2026-03-13","publisher":"ZooBotanica (SMC-Private) Limited","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":null,"container":{"name":"Planta Animalia","issnPrinted":"2958-6011","issnOnline":"2958-6003","issnLinking":null,"ep":"58","iss":null,"sp":"51","vol":"5","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::e9fc529d38d1cedadd1e333f7d827f8e","originalIds":["10.71454/pa.005.02.0369","50|doiboost____|e9fc529d38d1cedadd1e333f7d827f8e"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.71454/pa.005.02.0369"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":2.6085654E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Central Arid Zone Research Institute","acronym":"CAZRI","id":"openorgs____::8abddcb1c701637b04f5e79f26c4a12d","pids":[{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.464742.7"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q5060344"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000405046921"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q5060344"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"138570494"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04298em06"}],"countries":[{"code":"IN","label":"India"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.cazri.res.in/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.71454/pa.005.02.0369"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.71454/pa.005.02.0369"],"publicationDate":"2026-03-13","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn___print::648f7f89ce99cf3408f8939dee84ec89","value":"Planta Animalia"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Muhammad Fahad","name":"Muhammad","surname":"Fahad","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Syed Aftab Wajid","name":"Syed Aftab","surname":"Wajid","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::0330d9c18c5a8d42b509f051619221c6","fullName":"Ashfaq Ahmad","name":"Ashfaq","surname":"Ahmad","rank":3,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-9249-185x"},"provenance":null}},{"id":"orcid_______::1c859a4fdc85554a3370b79ce90ddda8","fullName":"Muhammad Jehanzeb Masud Cheema","name":"Muhammad Jehanzeb Masud","surname":"Cheema","rank":4,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-7911-7548"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Response of Wheat Cultivars to Deficit Irrigation under Semiarid Conditions of Faisalabad, Pakistan","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<ns2:p>Decreasing availability of water resources for crop production can be handled with rational and wise management of irrigation. Deficit irrigation could be a potential tool for irrigation management, but it requires a clear understanding of water movement in soil profile and crop response to water stress throughout the growing season. A field experiment was conducted to evaluate the response of wheat (Triticum aestivum L.) crop to deficit irrigation with analysis of soil water balance, soil moisture content and relationship between evapotranspiration and grain yield. Five different levels of deficit irrigation along with farmers’ irrigation practice [i.e., irrigation was applied at tillering, stem elongation, booting and grain formation stage I1(farmers’ practice at all growth stages), I2(farmers’ practice at all growth stages but no irrigation at stem elongation), I3(farmers’ practice at tillering and grain formation, while no irrigation at other growth stages), I4(farmers’ practice at tillering only and no irrigation at other growth stages), I5(50% of farmers’ practice at all growth stages) and I6(75% of farmers’ practice at all growth stages)] were applied to three wheat cultivars, i.e., Aas-2011, Galaxy-2013 and Punjab-2011. Experiment was conducted in winter seasons of 2014–2015 and 2015–2016 with three replications with strip plot arrangement under semi-arid conditions of Faisalabad. Grain yield was reduced by 35 to 44% when irrigation was skipped at booting and grain formation stage. Linear relationship was found between cumulative evapotranspiration and grain yield. The application of 75% of farmers’ irrigation practice (I6) saved 13 to 17% water and produced similar grain yield as acquired by farmers’ irrigation practice (I1). Therefore, it could be a potential strategy to cope with the drought period of crop from sowing to maturity under changing water scenarios of Faisalabad, Pakistan.</ns2:p>"],"publicationDate":"2025-01-01","publisher":"Friends Science Publishers","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"International Journal of Agriculture and Biology","issnPrinted":"1560-8530","issnOnline":null,"issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":null,"vol":"21","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::c31404fab3a432ac57350b78ada613eb","originalIds":["10.17957/ijab/15.0986","50|doiboost____|c31404fab3a432ac57350b78ada613eb"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.17957/ijab/15.0986"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":2.2497804E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":null,"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.17957/ijab/15.0986"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.17957/ijab/15.0986"],"publicationDate":"2025-01-01","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn___print::a8df70d61396efd0fa7dc8dd6b6a29f4","value":"International Journal of Agriculture and Biology"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":null,"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":"orcid_______::709f111067cd7998a1c19e3311ffd666","fullName":"O. I. Zhuk","name":"O. I.","surname":"Zhuk","rank":1,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0001-9429-2056"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"O. O. Stasik","name":"O. O.","surname":"Stasik","rank":2,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid_pending","value":"0000-0001-5023-2529"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Growth of shoots, ear and yield structure of winter wheat under drought","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Aim. The aim of the work was to study the growth of shoots, ears and the structure of the yield of bread winter wheat (Triticum aestivum L.) under drought. Methods. Wheat plants cultivars Chigyrynka and Jamala were grown under optimal conditions until the earing-flowering phase, after that the experimental plants were transferred to drought regime for 8 days. Optimal water supply was restored to the end of vegetation. During the experiment, shoot growth and ear weight were measured during the experiment. Ripened plants were analyzed the yield structure. Results. It was established that the water deficit in the soil during the critical phase of the ontogeny of earing-flowering delayed the growth of shoots, decreased the weight of the ear grain productivity in both cultivars of plants. Restoration of irrigation stimulated growth of shoots and ears, but did not compensate for the loss of yield. Conclusions. Water deficit in soil in critical earing-flowering phase decreased shoots length, ears weight and productivity of winter wheat plants.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2024-09-25","publisher":"Institute of Molecular Biology and Genetics (NAS Ukraine)","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Faktori eksperimental'noi evolucii organizmiv","issnPrinted":"2219-3782","issnOnline":"2415-3826","issnLinking":null,"ep":"28","iss":null,"sp":"23","vol":"35","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::60078cf898842dfb48a345fccb47e620","originalIds":["10.7124/feeo.v35.1653","50|doiboost____|60078cf898842dfb48a345fccb47e620"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.7124/feeo.v35.1653"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":1.0,"influence":2.2652378E-9,"popularity":2.609468E-9,"impulse":1.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Institute of Plant Physiology and Genetics of the National Academy of Sciences of Ukraine","acronym":"IPPG","id":"pending_org_::955113ae9ccc21f231e31c0fc398e713","pids":[{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q12074697"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04qaccc26"}],"countries":[{"code":"UA","label":"Ukraine"}],"websiteurl":"https://www.ifrg.kiev.ua"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.7124/feeo.v35.1653"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.7124/feeo.v35.1653"],"publicationDate":"2024-09-25","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::c5cafee4e804399c34ef748e9a6673c1","value":"Фактори експериментальної еволюції організмів"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Hosseini, Alireza Daneshvar","name":"Alireza Daneshvar","surname":"Hosseini","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Dadkhodaie, Ali","name":"Ali","surname":"Dadkhodaie","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Heidari, Bahram","name":"Bahram","surname":"Heidari","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Kazemeini, Seyed Abdolreza","name":"Seyed Abdolreza","surname":"Kazemeini","rank":4,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0106 biological sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"2. Zero hunger"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"15. Life on land"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"6. Clean water"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"[SHS.ANTHRO-BIO] Humanities and Social Sciences/Biological anthropology"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"3. Good health"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Evaluation of a Hexaploid Wheat Collection (Triticum aestivum L.) under Drought Stress Conditions Using Stress Tolerance Indices","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Wheat is the most important crop in the world which faces the global problem of drought. Its production is affected by water deficit after pollination in arid and semi-arid regions. An experiment was conducted to assess tolerance of 39 bread wheat genotypes to end-season drought. The experimental design was Randomized Complete Block in three replications and the drought tolerance indices (SSI, STI, TOL, MP and GMP) were calculated for grain yield. The cultivar Cambin produced the highest grain yield under normal irrigation by 369.19 g m-2 while Arina had the highest yield (223.35 g m-2) under drought stress conditions. Stress tolerance (TOL) introduced Hindukesh, Iran2355 and Iran6476 as drought tolerant genotypes. Also, results showed that grain yield under stress and non-stress environments were highly correlated with the mean productivity (MP), the geometric mean productivity (GMP) and tolerance index (TOL). These genotypes could be further used in crosses for genetic studies and breeding programs for improvement tolerance to drought.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2020-03-06","publisher":"Sciencedomain International","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":["Data, Forge"],"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Annual Research &amp; Review in Biology","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"2347-565X","issnLinking":null,"ep":"10","iss":null,"sp":"1","vol":null,"edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_dedup___::3b3bc20fbe7ee5dfe386c1f4150b9c4a","originalIds":["10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168","50|doiboost____|3b3bc20fbe7ee5dfe386c1f4150b9c4a","3010859577","oai:HAL:hal-05364593v1","50|od_______177::fd16a593bcde5c146d8a5b5ac21e2dcd"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":1.1111055E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Shiraz University","acronym":"Shiraz University","id":"openorgs____::3b4eb3c9cc69dc75e8513827f061da7b","pids":[{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/028qtbk54"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"100009587"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q2001798"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100005071"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100005071"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100007550"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"100009587"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.412573.6"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q2001798"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100007550"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"166459259"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"1398287"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000107451259"}],"countries":[{"code":"IR","label":"Iran (Islamic Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"http://shirazu.ac.ir/en/index.php/"},{"legalName":"Sher-e-Kashmir University of Agricultural Sciences and Technology of Kashmir","acronym":"SKUAST","id":"openorgs____::4d0bd8f364e8a61c6f3e456675a892db","pids":[{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000405006225"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.444725.4"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"1306687154"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q7494595"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"9426235"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/00jgwn197"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"232997476"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q7494595"}],"countries":[{"code":"IN","label":"India"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.skuastkashmir.ac.in/"},{"legalName":"University of Perugia","acronym":"University of Perugia","id":"openorgs____::c31cee02f54d59beb376b45f2467ea01","pids":[{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100010607"},{"scheme":"OrgReg","value":"IT0055"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q748085"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q748085"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"1946187"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000417573630"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100010607"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"27483092"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/00x27da85"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.9027.c"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"999846319"}],"countries":[{"code":"IT","label":"Italy"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.unipg.it/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"opendoar____::96da2f590cd7246bbde0051047b0d6f7","value":"Hyper Article en Ligne - Sciences de l'Homme et de la Société"},{"key":"openaire____::8ac8380272269217cb09a928c8caa993","value":"UnpayWall"},{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"},{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"],"publicationDate":"2020-03-06","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn__online::005fd19a265ce9a8c032a8d906fbee59","value":"Annual Research & Review in Biology"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}},{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://www.journalarrb.com/index.php/ARRB/article/download/30168/56627"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn__online::005fd19a265ce9a8c032a8d906fbee59","value":"Annual Research & Review in Biology"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::8ac8380272269217cb09a928c8caa993","value":"UnpayWall"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3010859577"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}},{"accessRight":{"code":"c_14cb","label":"CLOSED","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":null},"type":"Article","urls":["https://hal.science/hal-05364593v1"],"publicationDate":"2020-01-01","refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"opendoar____::96da2f590cd7246bbde0051047b0d6f7","value":"Hyper Article en Ligne - Sciences de l'Homme et de la Société"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"opendoar____::96da2f590cd7246bbde0051047b0d6f7","value":"Hyper Article en Ligne - Sciences de l'Homme et de la Société"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3010859577"},{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.9734/arrb/2019/v34i630168"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Wojciech Pikuła","name":"Wojciech","surname":"Pikuła","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::5731fd0a43ae090814b7bcf3e9bd87e9","fullName":"Marta Jańczak-Pieniążek","name":"Marta","surname":"Jańczak-Pieniążek","rank":2,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0003-4655-7974"},"provenance":null}},{"id":"orcid_______::6e945fe07d6b5ec225c258cf512144c0","fullName":"Ewa Szpunar-Krok","name":"Ewa","surname":"Szpunar-Krok","rank":3,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0001-5788-3631"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Role of Nitrogen Fertilization in Mitigating Drought-Induced Physiological Stress in Wheat Seedlings","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Drought stress is one of the major abiotic factors limiting crop growth and yield, particularly in wheat. Water deficit leads to reduced chlorophyll content, impaired photosynthetic performance, and decreased biomass accumulation. Nitrogen fertilization may influence plant physiological responses to drought; however, its capacity to alleviate drought-induced growth reduction remains uncertain. A pot experiment was conducted to evaluate the impact of different nitrogen-based fertilizers on wheat seedlings grown under irrigation level 60% PPW (control) and 30% PPW (drought stress) conditions, with balanced levels of phosphorus and potassium maintained in all treatments. Water deficit led to substantial reductions in chlorophyll content compared to optimally irrigated plants. Similarly, the performance index (PI) decreased by 139.3% at Term 1 (1 day after foliar nitrogen application) and 27.2% at Term 2 (7 days after application). The net photosynthetic rate (Pn) declined markedly under drought conditions and was not significantly improved by nitrogen fertilization, indicating a partial and mainly short-term physiological response to nitrogen under water deficit. The application of nitrogen fertilizers, particularly urea and Nitron S, modulated the relative chlorophyll content and selected chlorophyll fluorescence (Fv/Fm, Fv/Fo, PI) and gas-exchange (E, gs, Ci) parameters under drought conditions, mainly shortly after application. However, aboveground dry biomass under drought conditions was not significantly affected by any nitrogen fertilizer. Urea induced the most consistent short-term physiological responses under both irrigation regimes, with effects more pronounced shortly after application, whereas Nitron S showed fertilizer-specific effects under drought stress. Overall, the results demonstrate that foliar nitrogen fertilization can modulate short-term physiological responses of wheat seedlings to drought but does not translate into sustained improvements in Pn or biomass accumulation. In the context of climate change and increasing water scarcity, identifying nitrogen fertilizers that support physiological functioning without overestimating growth benefits has critical implications for sustainable wheat production. Optimizing nitrogen fertilization may, therefore, contribute to improved nutrient management strategies under water-limited conditions.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2026-01-29","publisher":"MDPI AG","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Agriculture","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"2077-0472","issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":"337","vol":"16","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::43ae9f3bc67a04c3fbd5f9ff9ff94195","originalIds":["agriculture16030337","10.3390/agriculture16030337","50|doiboost____|43ae9f3bc67a04c3fbd5f9ff9ff94195"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/agriculture16030337"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":1.0,"influence":2.2385878E-9,"popularity":3.267182E-9,"impulse":1.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C4"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Rzeszów University","acronym":"Rzeszów University","id":"openorgs____::ab127fb7c2727dd8143c125038a14afb","pids":[{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100007817"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100014147"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000121543176"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q660907"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"984448830"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/03pfsnq21"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q660907"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"11736035"},{"scheme":"OrgReg","value":"PL0207"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.13856.39"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100007817"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100014147"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"98559091"}],"countries":[{"code":"PL","label":"Poland"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.ur.edu.pl/en"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/agriculture16030337"}],"license":"CC BY","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.3390/agriculture16030337"],"publicationDate":"2026-01-29","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::617d99ca6079e7b9498eb7d9ee02f577","value":"Agriculture"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"S. Nassar","name":"S.","surname":"Nassar","rank":1,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0106 biological sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"2. Zero hunger"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"15. Life on land"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"6. Clean water"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"3. Good health"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Selection for Earliness in Two Wheat Populations under Toshka Conditions","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["Drought is a significant constraint for defecting yield and output potential as growth and productivity of plants are adversely affected by water stress which leads to heavy yield losses. A good drought tolerant wheat line should have the ability to withstand in water deficit conditions or have the ability to escape from it. The present work aimed to study the effect of two cycles of pedigree selection for earliness in two segregating populations of wheat under water stress, during 2015/16 and 2016/17 season at Toshka Station, Desert Research Center, Aswan, Egypt. The water treatments were 100 and 67% of the irrigation requirements of wheat in Toshka as normal and drought condition, respectively. The selected family No. 12 was the earlier family and recorded 55.33 and 48.33 days under normal and drought stress, respectively. Moreover, family No. 10 was the best family that showed high grain yield under both water irrigations in Pop.1. Drought stress led to reduce grain yield/plant (GYPP) by 35.3% in best family. These families could be considered as tolerant to drought stress and could be used in breeding program for drought tolerance and earliness. In Pop.2, family No. 6 was the best selected one in (GYPP, BYPP, GYPS, NGPS and 100-GW) under normal conditions, while the family No. 7 was the best one for days to heading under normal and drought stress conditions. The performance of the selected families under normal and/or drought stress conditions may determine the goals and attitudes of the selection program."],"publicationDate":"2020-03-01","publisher":"Egypts Presidential Specialized Council for Education and Scientific Research","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Journal of Plant Production","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"2090-374X","issnLinking":null,"ep":"240","iss":null,"sp":"233","vol":"11","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_dedup___::736d4eef0cd4bdcec1db93fccb19b240","originalIds":["10.21608/jpp.2020.87101","50|doiboost____|736d4eef0cd4bdcec1db93fccb19b240","3024921084"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":1.1111055E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Desert Research Center","acronym":"DRC","id":"openorgs____::de55e9e64c5d730f46e1eb53c60a3f3e","pids":[{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04dzf3m45"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"893194237"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.466634.5"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000453739159"}],"countries":[{"code":"EG","label":"Egypt"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.drc.gov.eg/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::8ac8380272269217cb09a928c8caa993","value":"UnpayWall"},{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"},{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"],"publicationDate":"2020-03-01","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::56ea413e61f6ce295aa26f7bb56f8193","value":"Journal of Plant Production"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}},{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"bronze"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://jpp.journals.ekb.eg/article_87101_cf8333604d977619b23eed687b066376.pdf"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::56ea413e61f6ce295aa26f7bb56f8193","value":"Journal of Plant Production"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::8ac8380272269217cb09a928c8caa993","value":"UnpayWall"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3024921084"},{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"}],"type":"Other literature type","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"3024921084"},{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"}],"type":"Other literature type","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.21608/jpp.2020.87101"],"refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"openaire____::55045bd2a65019fd8e6741a755395c8c","value":"Unknown Repository"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::5f532a3fc4f1ea403f37070f59a7a53a","value":"Microsoft Academic Graph"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":"orcid_______::11a5287a79b48267bccda51926328e36","fullName":"Hebat-Allah A. Hussein","name":"Hebat-Allah A.","surname":"Hussein","rank":1,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0001-8794-7675"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"Shifaa O. Alshammari","name":"Shifaa O.","surname":"Alshammari","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Marwa E. Abd El-Sadek","name":"Marwa E.","surname":"Abd El-Sadek","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Sahar K. M. Kenawy","name":"Sahar K. M.","surname":"Kenawy","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::680bebacf2905e937f61968ddbf8a8f8","fullName":"Ali A. Badawy","name":"Ali A.","surname":"Badawy","rank":5,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-0422-8091"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0301 basic medicine"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"2. Zero hunger"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0303 health sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"osmolytes"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"pigments"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"Agriculture (General)"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"drought"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"phenols"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"15. Life on land"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"yield"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"SDG","value":"6. Clean water"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"S1-972"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"03 medical and health sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"polyamine"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"wheat"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"flavonoids"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"protein"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"The Promotive Effect of Putrescine on Growth, Biochemical Constituents, and Yield of Wheat (Triticum aestivum L.) Plants under Water Stress","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Drought stress is a significant environmental variable affecting wheat growth and development. Plant stress tolerance is intimately related to growth regulators of plants as polyamines. The study assessed the impact of drought (50% water irrigation and 100% water irrigation), priming of grains in putrescine (0.25, 0.5, and 1 mM), and their interactions on the growth, yield, and physiological attributes of wheat plants. Drought conditions declined plant height, fresh and dry weights, leaves and tillers numbers, and flag leaf area. However, applying putrescine, especially at (1 mM), enhanced wheat growth performance in normal or water-deficit conditions. Drought stress decreased spike length (28.6%), number of spikelets (15.6%), number of grains (30.3%), the weight of the spike (23.5%), and the weight of the grains/spike (37.5%). In addition, drought decreased the contents of chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, free amino acids, and total phenols, while applying putrescine enhanced wheat plant growth performance in normal or drought conditions. Putrescine at (1 mM) achieved the highest increase in plant height (38.8%), root length (50%), leaves number (166%), tillers number (80%), flag leaf area (70.3%), shoot fresh weight (99.4%), shoot dry weight (98.4%), root fresh weight (97.8%), root dry weight (210%) compared to the untreated plants. Moreover, pretreatment with putrescine improved chlorophyll a (13.3%), chlorophyll b (70.3%), carotenoids (61.8%), soluble sugars (49.1%), amino acids (42.7%), phenols (52.4%), number of spikelets (59.3%), number of grains (81.1%), and weight of spike (45.4%). Moreover, variations in the protein profile of wheat plants were due to drought conditions and putrescine application. In conclusion, priming wheat grains with putrescine effectively induces protective mechanisms against water stress and improves wheat plants’ physiological attributes and yield components.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2023-02-28","publisher":"MDPI AG","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref","Agriculture, Vol 13, Iss 3, p 587 (2023)","Agriculture","Volume 13","Issue 3","Pages: 587"],"formats":["application/pdf"],"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Agriculture","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"2077-0472","issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":"587","vol":"13","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_dedup___::23c3a27e71d65be43633d2480ebbbeae","originalIds":["agriculture13030587","10.3390/agriculture13030587","50|doiboost____|23c3a27e71d65be43633d2480ebbbeae","oai:doaj.org/article:6a84d0643044496895a8fb93bb8e1526","50|doajarticles::06794d0335bfc0a9635f4c821dd42ac6","50|multidiscipl::f21b766e3e004d72118324a24a3c450c","oai:mdpi.com:/2077-0472/13/3/587/"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/agriculture13030587"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":47.0,"influence":3.135076E-9,"popularity":3.239863E-8,"impulse":47.0,"citationClass":"C4","influenceClass":"C4","impulseClass":"C3","popularityClass":"C3"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Faculty of Science, Tanta University","acronym":"Faculty of Science, Tanta University","id":"pending_org_::56db351f4fc8549b9daaf225cea4914e","pids":null,"countries":[{"code":"EG","label":"Egypt"}],"websiteurl":null},{"legalName":"University of Hafr Al-Batin","acronym":"UOHB","id":"openorgs____::e011712760df9c7e708cb6ffd2d67fdf","pids":[{"scheme":"fundref","value":"100017485"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.494617.9"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/021jt1927"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000449078298"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q27156930"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"100017485"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q27156930"}],"countries":[{"code":"SA","label":"Saudi Arabia"}],"websiteurl":"https://www.uohb.edu.sa/portal/en/"},{"legalName":"Al Azhar University","acronym":"Al Azhar University","id":"openorgs____::61600bda73fd66d056c502612938f3d1","pids":[{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q173959"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.411303.4"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000121556022"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100018778"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q173959"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"184834183"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/05fnp1145"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100018778"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"274751"}],"countries":[{"code":"EG","label":"Egypt"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.azhar.edu.eg/En/u.htm"},{"legalName":"The Open University of Japan","acronym":"OUJ","id":"openorgs____::fe0cfba0a5f42c26dbab0508ff8499eb","pids":[{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.412875.d"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"7735478"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/03jyr9x65"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"203189479"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000086676925"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q15501569"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q15501569"}],"countries":[{"code":"JP","label":"Japan"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.ouj.ac.jp/eng/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::c2cdfa5866e03cdd07d313cbc8fb8311","value":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute"},{"key":"driver______::bee53aa31dc2cbb538c10c2b65fa5824","value":"DOAJ"},{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/agriculture13030587"}],"license":"CC BY","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.3390/agriculture13030587"],"publicationDate":"2023-02-28","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::617d99ca6079e7b9498eb7d9ee02f577","value":"Agriculture"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/agriculture13030587"}],"accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doaj.org/article/6a84d0643044496895a8fb93bb8e1526"],"publicationDate":"2023-02-01","refereed":"nonPeerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::617d99ca6079e7b9498eb7d9ee02f577","value":"Agriculture"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"driver______::bee53aa31dc2cbb538c10c2b65fa5824","value":"DOAJ"}},{"alternateIdentifiers":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.3390/agriculture13030587"}],"license":"CC BY","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"gold"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://dx.doi.org/10.3390/agriculture13030587"],"publicationDate":"2023-02-28","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"doajarticles::617d99ca6079e7b9498eb7d9ee02f577","value":"Agriculture"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::c2cdfa5866e03cdd07d313cbc8fb8311","value":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"green":false,"inDiamondJournal":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Muhammad Usman","name":"Muhammad","surname":"Usman","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Mudassir Aziz","name":"Mudassir","surname":"Aziz","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Muhammad Mansoor Javaid","name":"Muhammad Mansoor","surname":"Javaid","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Athar Mahmood","name":"Athar","surname":"Mahmood","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Salman Alamery","name":"Salman","surname":"Alamery","rank":5,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::13ffe628d13bc0afc89009ec582e07b2","fullName":"Sajid Fiaz","name":"Sajid","surname":"Fiaz","rank":6,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0001-9097-4359"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"Khuram Mubeen","name":"Khuram","surname":"Mubeen","rank":7,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Tauqeer Ahmad Yasir","name":"Tauqeer Ahmad","surname":"Yasir","rank":8,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Muhammd Ather Nadeem","name":"Muhammd Ather","surname":"Nadeem","rank":9,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::721f7b9f1250c3c4ca30d074c62a49c4","fullName":"Kotb A. Attia","name":"Kotb A.","surname":"Attia","rank":10,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-2992-1765"},"provenance":null}},{"id":null,"fullName":"Yaser M Hafez","name":"Yaser M","surname":"Hafez","rank":11,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::f29fc777d013891452e21f36cc538303","fullName":"Muhammad Nadeem","name":"Muhammad","surname":"Nadeem","rank":12,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-4246-1791"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":"gold","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"und","label":"Undetermined"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0401 agriculture, forestry, and fisheries"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"04 agricultural and veterinary sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Gas Exchange Characteristics, Water Relations, and Irrigation Water Use Efficiency of Wheat Influenced by Potassium Nutrition Under Water-Limited Conditions","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:p>Potassium is an essential nutrient that contributes to the survival of wheat once subjected to water deficit conditions. A field trial was conducted using a randomized complete block design with splitplot arrangements. Different water regimes, including well-watered and water-deficit conditions at the tillering stage, were maintained in the main plot. Subplot treatments included no potassium; soil applied the recommended dose of potassium, distilled water spray, 1% potassium spray, and 2% potassium spray, respectively. A significantly higher value of gas exchange characteristics, water relations, and wheat yield was recorded with the foliar application of potassium than those of the control. The foliar spray of 2% potassium was superior to soil-applied potash under water deficit conditions, with a 25.33% improvement in grain yield of wheat. Similarly, a 47.58% and 25.76% improvement in grain yield of wheat was recorded with 2% and 1% potassium spray as compared to that of distilled water spray under water deficit conditions. The chlorophyll content, cell membrane stability, relative water content, net photosynthetic rate, stomatal conductance, and transpiration rate were 21.5%, 26.07%, 2.79%, 84.41%, 13.84%, and 10%, respectively. Irrigation water use efficiency under drought conditions was 51.02% higher with a 2% foliar spray of potash than in well-watered conditions where potassium was not applied. The benefit-cost ratio was recorded as 1.24 and 1.30 with 2% foliar application of potassium and soil-applied potassium, respectively. Our findings displayed that a 2% foliar spray of potash can be utilized under water deficit conditions for improvement of grain yield, whereas soil application of potash can be utilized with additional benefits.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2025-09-01","publisher":"HARD Publishing Company","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-r